#!/usr/bin/python3
"""``./verify`` -- everything about this game that a machine can check, in one
command.

    ./verify                 the three stages below
    ./verify --corner        and the real-window measurement as a fourth
    ./verify --only play     just one stage, by its short name
    ./verify --list          the stages, their short names, and what each proves

Three stages run by default, in this order, cheapest first:

=========  ==========================================================
``suite``  the whole test suite, at the one pinned command:
           ``/usr/bin/python3 -m unittest discover -s tests``
``smoke``  ``./launch-smoke`` -- WI-8's one-command launch smoke, run as
           the separate program it is. **It opens a real Terminal window
           on the screen** and closes it again.
``play``   three scripted play-throughs that drive the real loop with a
           fake screen and a fake clock, through the real rules and the
           real renderer, and compare the **final picture** with a
           recorded one, character for character.
=========  ==========================================================

**A harness that cannot say what broke is not a harness.** Every stage has a
name, a one-line statement of what it proves, and its own captured output, and
a failure prints all three under a banner that says which stage of how many
failed. Nothing here reports "verification failed" and leaves you to guess.

**What it deliberately does not do.** It cannot press a key, see the screen, or
know which window a person was looking at -- no agent in this project has a
controlling tty. Six things therefore remain a person's, and they are packaged
one step each in ``docs/findings/WI-10-human-checks.md``. ``./verify`` passing
is not the same as the game being right; it is the half of "right" that can be
re-checked in eleven seconds.

**The window rules (implementation plan §2.6) apply to two of these stages.**
``smoke`` is a separate program and looks after its own window. ``corner``
opens one here: it captures the id from the supervisor at the instant the
window is created, addresses that id and no other, lets the child exit by
itself, confirms it has gone with ``visible`` rather than ``exists``, and reaps
it in a ``finally`` even when the stage fails. It never closes a busy tab --
Terminal's modal confirmation sheet can only be dismissed by a human, and while
it is up it blocks every AppleScript call in the system.
"""

